{"body":"@Soph, I think this PR should land before #2131, even if we assume both branches are clean and ready to merge.\r\n\r\nThe reason is architectural ordering, not conflict resolution. This PR makes `gitrepo.ResolveWorktreeMetadata` the single owner of `.git`, `gitdir:`, `commondir`, and linked-worktree identity. It also removes the separate checkpoint and session traversal code and their caches.\r\n\r\n#2131 changes checkpoint persistence by adding CAS-based ref transactions and batch writers. Parts of that code still need the worktree root or common Git directory for native `git update-ref` calls and shadow-ref handling. If this PR lands first, #2131 has a clear rule to follow: `gitrepo` resolves physical repository metadata, while `checkpoint` owns ref publication and retries. That separation should also prevent the batching work from preserving or reintroducing another metadata traversal path.\r\n\r\nWe could merge #2131 first, but then this PR would need to absorb and migrate its new transaction code while already changing metadata ownership across the repository. That would mix two separate review questions: who owns Git metadata, and how checkpoint refs are published safely under concurrent writers.\r\n\r\nMy preferred order is:\r\n\r\n1. Merge this PR.\r\n2. Rebase #2131 onto it and migrate any remaining `resolveGitCommonDir` calls to `ResolveWorktreeMetadata`.\r\n3. Rerun the batching and CAS concurrency tests, followed by the hook performance tests.\r\n\r\nDo you see a dependency that argues for merging #2131 first?","created_at":"2026-09-01T13:01:52Z","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2190#issuecomment-5494343752","updated_at":"2026-09-01T13:02:26Z","user":"MuskanPaliwal"}
